#+ Washington TBs and POIs localization.
#@{
	key[]: "tb_wa1"
	val[]: "The Olympic Peninsula"

	key[]: "tb_wa1_desc"
	val[]: "The Olympic Peninsula reveals a place where ocean, forest, and mountains coexist in ever-changing beauty. Mist drifts over rocky shores, while waterfalls tumble from the highlands, where hidden valleys shelter small historic towns."

	key[]: "poi_wa1_01"
	val[]: "Historic United States Post Office"

	key[]: "poi_wa1_01_desc"
	val[]: "Completed in 1893 on a bluff above Port Townsend’s waterfront, the building was designed as a federal facility with a customhouse, reflecting the city’s early ambitions as an emerging international trade hub."

	key[]: "poi_wa1_02"
	val[]: "Port Angeles"

	key[]: "poi_wa1_02_desc"
	val[]: "Port Angeles is a coastal city founded in the 19th century as a harbour and supply point. Today, it serves as a key gateway to Olympic National Park and a ferry port linking the United States and Canada."

	key[]: "poi_wa1_03"
	val[]: "Lake Crescent"

	key[]: "poi_wa1_03_desc"
	val[]: "Lake Crescent, Washington’s second-deepest lake, is known for its vivid blue, crystal-clear waters, a result of low nitrogen levels. Set within a popular recreational area, it is surrounded by trails and old-growth forest."
	
	key[]: "poi_wa1_04"
	val[]: "Kalaloch Beach"

	key[]: "poi_wa1_04_desc"
	val[]: "Kalaloch Beach is known for the “Tree of Life,” a Sitka spruce with exposed roots spanning a small creek to the shore. Unlike typical tall spruces, it is shorter, thicker, and windswept, making it a unique and popular local landmark."

	key[]: "poi_wa1_05"
	val[]: "Olympic National Park"

	key[]: "poi_wa1_05_desc"
	val[]: "Olympic National Park protects diverse ecosystems, from a rugged Pacific coastline and temperate rainforests to alpine peaks. First classified as a National Monument in 1909, it was renamed a National Park by the federal government in 1938."

	key[]: "poi_wa1_06"
	val[]: "Greenhead Slough"

	key[]: "poi_wa1_06_desc"
	val[]: "Greenhead Slough is a small inlet and wetland channel along Highway 101 near Willapa Bay. It lies within a coastal estuary habitat that has been restored to support salmon spawning and abundant waterfowl viewing."

	key[]: "poi_wa1_07"
	val[]: "Dabob Bay"

	key[]: "poi_wa1_07_desc"
	val[]: "Dabob Bay is a deep fjord that supports salmon, shellfish and wildlife. The Dabob Bay Natural Area, established in 1984, protects rare salt marsh and sand-spit plant communities within a highly functional tidal wetland system."


##########################################################################################

	key[]: "tb_wa2"
	val[]: "The Heartland"

	key[]: "tb_wa2_desc"
	val[]: "Washington’s heartland invites travellers along picturesque roads through evergreen forests and calm alpine lakes. Mount Rainier dominates the horizon, offering iconic views and a peaceful journey into the natural landscapes of the Pacific Northwest."

	key[]: "poi_wa2_01"
	val[]: "Mount Rainier"

	key[]: "poi_wa2_01_desc"
	val[]: "Mount Rainier, Washington’s tallest peak at 14,411 feet, is an active stratovolcano. Its glaciers feed rivers, shape valleys, and sustain rich forests and wildlife, making it a vital natural landmark."

	key[]: "poi_wa2_02"
	val[]: "Wenatchee National Forest"

	key[]: "poi_wa2_02_desc"
	val[]: "Wenatchee National Forest spans mountains, rivers, and alpine valleys. In 1933, the Civilian Conservation Corps built trails and fire lookouts here, leaving a lasting mark on the forest’s recreational and conservation history."

	key[]: "poi_wa2_03"
	val[]: "Mount Baker-Snoqualmie National Forest"

	key[]: "poi_wa2_03_desc"
	val[]: "Mount Baker-Snoqualmie National Forest hosts some of the oldest glacier-monitoring sites in America, tracking volcanic ice flows and climate changes across the Cascades since the early 1900s."
	
	key[]: "poi_wa2_04"
	val[]: "Wenatchee River"

	key[]: "poi_wa2_04_desc"
	val[]: "Along the Wenatchee River, remnants of early 19th-century irrigation canals and mills show how settlers harnessed its waters. Native tribes also relied on its salmon-rich waters for food and trade long before European settlement."

	key[]: "poi_wa2_05"
	val[]: "Klawatti Peak"

	key[]: "poi_wa2_05_desc"
	val[]: "First ascended in the mid-20th century, Klawatti Peak remains rarely climbed due to its steep, glacier-covered slopes, making it a coveted challenge for mountaineers seeking isolation in the North Cascades."

	key[]: "poi_wa2_06"
	val[]: "Dry Falls Dam"

	key[]: "poi_wa2_06_desc"
	val[]: "Constructed to manage local waterways, Dry Falls Dam sits atop prehistoric basalt cliffs, controlling seasonal flows while revealing terraces and plunge pools carved by Ice Age floods."

	key[]: "poi_wa2_07"
	val[]: "White Mountain Fire Overlook"

	key[]: "poi_wa2_07_desc"
	val[]: "The overlook features interpretive signs about the 1988 White Mountain Fire, one of Washington’s largest wildfires in recent history, revealing scars and regrowth patterns in the forest."

	key[]: "wa_cut_helen_desc"
	val[]: "Before 1980, Mount St. Helens had a conical shape and was referred to as the Mount Fuji of America. The 1980 eruption removed 1,300 ft of its summit, forming a horseshoe crater now partly filled by a lava dome and glacier."


##########################################################################################


	key[]: "tb_wa3"
	val[]: "The Wine Country"

	key[]: "tb_wa3_desc"
	val[]: "East of the Cascades, Washington’s Wine Country spans the Columbia Valley AVA. The region includes Selah Creek, Lewis and Clark State Park, and lands historically inhabited by Indigenous peoples, featuring dry, sunny landscapes with rolling hills and river valleys."

	key[]: "poi_wa3_01"
	val[]: "Clear Creek Falls"

	key[]: "poi_wa3_01_desc"
	val[]: "Clear Creek Falls Overlook is a popular stop just east of White Pass Summit. About a mile further east, the turn-off leads to Clear Lake Recreation Area, known for fishing and excellent opportunities to observe local wildlife."

	key[]: "poi_wa3_02"
	val[]: "Selah Creek Bridge"

	key[]: "poi_wa3_02_desc"
	val[]: "The Fred G. Redmon Bridge, or Selah Creek Bridge, is a twin-arch structure in Yakima County. When completed, it was the longest concrete-arch bridge in America and the highest automobile bridge in the state."

	key[]: "poi_wa3_03"
	val[]: "Grape Fields"
	
	key[]: "poi_wa3_03_desc"
	val[]: "Washington’s valleys and fertile soil support many grapevines, including Riesling, Cabernet and Merlot. The vineyards are an important part of the state’s agriculture and local landscapes."

	key[]: "poi_wa3_04"
	val[]: "Ed Hendler Bridge"

	key[]: "poi_wa3_04_desc"
	val[]: "Built in 1978 and sometimes called the Intercity Bridge, this bridge crosses the Columbia River between Pasco and Kennewick in southeastern Washington. Since its opening, it has been regarded as a landmark and symbol of the Tri-Cities."

	key[]: "poi_wa3_05"
	val[]: "Lewis and Clark State Park"

	key[]: "poi_wa3_05_desc"
	val[]: "One of Washington’s first state parks, Lewis and Clark State Park preserves ancient forests along a historic Indigenous route. Named to honour the Corps of Discovery, the forests recall the dense woodlands Meriwether Lewis described in his journals."

	key[]: "poi_wa3_06"
	val[]: "Columbia County Courthouse"

	key[]: "poi_wa3_06_desc"
	val[]: "The Columbia County Courthouse reflects a time when county government was central to daily life. During a period of rapid growth and limited contact with the state capital, courthouses served as the main source of authority and addressed citizens’ immediate needs."

	key[]: "poi_wa3_07"
	val[]: "Native American Tribes"

	key[]: "poi_wa3_07_desc"
	val[]: "The Columbia Valley and surrounding areas were home to Indigenous peoples, including the Yakama, Walla Walla and Umatilla tribes. Today, these communities continue to live in the region, maintaining cultural traditions and managing land and water resources."

##########################################################################################

#@}




#+ Oregon TBs and POIs localization.
#@{
	key[]: "tb_or1"
	val[]: "The Pacific Coast"

	key[]: "tb_or1_desc"
	val[]: "Oregon’s Pacific Coast unfolds through rugged headlands with scenic viewpoints. As Heceta Head Lighthouse overlooks crashing waves, Silver Point, Ophir Wayside, Sisters Rocks, and Kissing Rock rise with basalt cliffs to meet the Pacific surf along the way."

	key[]: "landmark_or_astoria_b_desc"
	val[]: "A four-mile span across the Columbia River, this bridge links Oregon and Washington, offering views of the river’s mouth and Pacific horizon."

	key[]: "poi_or1_01"
	val[]: "Silver Point Interpretive Overlook"

	key[]: "poi_or1_01_desc"
	val[]: "Just south of Cannon Beach, this overlook gives sweeping views of Haystack Rock and the rugged coastline, especially stunning at sunset."

	key[]: "or_cut_lighthouse_desc"
	val[]: "Oregon’s tallest lighthouse, built in 1873, stands on a basalt headland near Newport."

	key[]: "or_cut_newport_desc"
	val[]: "The Yaquina Bay Bridge, completed in 1936, is one of Oregon’s most iconic coastal landmarks. Designed by renowned engineer Conde McCullough, its elegant arch spans Yaquina Bay and remains a defining feature of U.S. Route 101."

	key[]: "or_cut_thor_desc"
	val[]: "A natural sinkhole on Cape Perpetua, where surging waves collapse, giving the illusion that the ocean is draining away."

	key[]: "poi_or1_02"
	val[]: "Ophir Wayside"

	key[]: "poi_or1_02_desc"
	val[]: "A quiet pull-off near Gold Beach, where visitors can stretch, watch the surf, or comb the beach for driftwood and agates."

	key[]: "poi_or1_03"
	val[]: "Sisters Rock Overlook"

	key[]: "poi_or1_03_desc"
	val[]: "Dramatic offshore sea stacks rise from the surf, with trails leading down to pocket beaches hidden between cliffs."

	key[]: "poi_or1_04"
	val[]: "Kissing Rock"

	key[]: "poi_or1_04_desc"
	val[]: "A roadside stop south of Gold Beach, where two massive rock formations lean together above the sand, making it a favourite photo spot."


##########################################################################################

	key[]: "tb_or2"
	val[]: "The Wild East"

	key[]: "tb_or2_desc"
	val[]: "Often called Oregon’s Wild East, this region reveals landscapes shaped by geologic forces over hundreds of millions of years, many still active today. Against that vast timescale, human history is brief, yet marked by moments as dramatic as the land itself."

	key[]: "landmark_or_deadman_p_desc"
	val[]: "Near Pendleton, this lookout area offers views of Deadman Pass, named for an 1870s incident during the Bannock War when a wagon driver was found dead. Decades earlier, countless wagons crossed these hills on the Oregon Trail."

	key[]: "or_cut_395_desc"
	val[]: "Once known as the Three Flags Highway, US-395 stretched from Canada to Mexico, linking deserts, forests, and mountains. Today it ends at I-15 in California, though remnants of the original route remain near San Diego."

	key[]: "poi_or2_01"
	val[]: "Malheur National Forest"

	key[]: "poi_or2_01_desc"
	val[]: "Malheur National Forest lies in the Blue Mountains. More than 150 million years ago, some of the oldest rocks in this region formed part of volcanic islands in the Pacific, later merging with the continent."
	
	key[]: "poi_or2_02"
	val[]: "Badlands Wilderness"

	key[]: "poi_or2_02_desc"
	val[]: "Badlands Wilderness, just east of Bend, is a rugged volcanic landscape shaped by ancient lava flows and windblown ash. Its twisted lava formations, sandy soils, and stark terrain create one of the state's most unique high-desert environments."

	key[]: "poi_or2_03"
	val[]: "Lake Abert"

	key[]: "poi_or2_03_desc"
	val[]: "First mapped in 1843, Lake Abert has supported human presence for over 10,000 years. Though too saline for fish, it remains a vital stop for birds along the Pacific Flyway and has been shrinking in recent decades due to climate change."

	key[]: "poi_or2_04"
	val[]: "East Steens Tour Route"

	key[]: "poi_or2_04_desc"
	val[]: "The East Steens Tour Route follows Highway 78 along Steens Mountain and the Alvord Desert. Its highlight is the Alvord Desert playa, one of Oregon’s largest dry lakebeds, which shifts seasonally between a shallow lake and a vast desert plain."

	key[]: "poi_or2_05"
	val[]: "Doherty Slide"

	key[]: "poi_or2_05_desc"
	val[]: "It takes less than an hour to drive along the Doherty Slide, a rock formation born of volcanic activity. Yet it took nature around 16 million years to shape the landscape as it appears today."


##########################################################################################

	key[]: "tb_or3"
	val[]: "The Ogden State"

	key[]: "tb_or3_desc"
	val[]: "This route follows Oregon’s inland north–south corridor, passing forests and high desert. First explored by fur trader Peter Skene Ogden in the 1820s, it later gained strategic importance during the Second World War for its timber and transport routes."
	
	key[]: "poi_or3_01"
	val[]: "Springfield Flame"

	key[]: "poi_or3_01_desc"
	val[]: "Standing nearly 65 feet tall, the sculpture marks Springfield’s northern gateway, designed as a flame to serve as a beacon for travellers and a landmark for residents and visitors alike."

	key[]: "poi_or3_02"
	val[]: "Santiam Wagon Road"

	key[]: "poi_or3_02_desc"
	val[]: "The pass east of here, once called Wiley Pass after Andrew Wiley, was explored in 1859 by pioneers seeking grazing routes. A military road was planned in 1864, but remained largely unbuilt. A toll route, the Santiam Wagon Road, operated from 1868 to 1914."

	key[]: "poi_or3_03"
	val[]: "Veteran Historic Highway"

	key[]: "poi_or3_03_desc"
	val[]: "At the highway’s northern gateway, the final two of 18 tribute signs were installed on 9 November 2012, south of Biggs Junction. They mark Camp Rufus as the northern entry point to the nation’s first highway dedicated to Second World War veterans."

	key[]: "poi_or3_04"
	val[]: "Ogden State Scenic Viewpoint"

	key[]: "poi_or3_04_desc"
	val[]: "Named for Peter Skene Ogden, an early 19th-century explorer and Hudson’s Bay Company chief trader, the wayside honours his expeditions across the Snake River country and his role among the first to explore the Great Salt Lake Basin."

	key[]: "landmark_or_fremont_b_desc"
	val[]: "The bridge and Portland’s Fremont Street are named for John C. Frémont, an early explorer of Las Californias and the Oregon Country. It features Oregon’s longest main span and ranks as the world’s second-longest tied-arch bridge."

	
##########################################################################################

#@}



#+ California TBs and POIs localization.
#@{
	key[]: "tb_ca1"
	val[]: "The Golden Horizon"

	key[]: "tb_ca1_desc"
	val[]: "Iconic bridges rise above shimmering waters, linking Alcatraz, Santa Cruz, and the region’s hills and neighbourhoods. From bustling streets and historic sites to sunlit beaches and scenic coastlines, the Bay Area offers a California experience like no other."

	key[]: "ca_cut_golden_gate_bridge_desc"
	val[]: "The bridge linking San Francisco Bay to the Pacific Ocean is an iconic symbol of San Francisco and California. When it opened in 1937, it held the records for both the world’s longest and tallest suspension bridge."

	key[]: "poi_ca1_01"
	val[]: "Alcatraz"

	key[]: "poi_ca1_01_desc"
	val[]: "Alcatraz Island was van 1934 tot 1963 de thuisbasis van een federale gevangenis. Het eiland stond bekend om het huisvesten van enkele van de meest beruchte criminelen van het land en is nu een historische plek en een populaire toeristische bestemming."

	key[]: "ca_cut_richmond_desc"
	val[]: "The Richmond–San Rafael Bridge spans San Francisco Bay, linking Richmond and San Rafael. Opened in 1956, it carries traffic across the northern bay and features a cantilever and truss design, serving as a key regional transport link."
	
	key[]: "poi_ca1_02"
	val[]: "Treasure Island"

	key[]: "poi_ca1_02_desc"
	val[]: "Treasure Island is a man-made island built for the Golden Gate International Exposition in 1939. After serving as a naval base, it is now being redeveloped for residential and commercial use."

	key[]: "poi_ca1_03"
	val[]: "Surfing City"

	key[]: "poi_ca1_03_desc"
	val[]: "The Surfing Statue in Santa Cruz honours the city’s surfing heritage and culture. Installed near the beachfront, it celebrates local surfers and the sport that has shaped Santa Cruz’s identity and coastal community."

	key[]: "poi_ca1_04"
	val[]: "Madonna Inn"

	key[]: "poi_ca1_04_desc"
	val[]: "A former popular restaurant and event landmark at the top of Highway 152. Closed since 2004 due to highway issues, it remains a frequent topic in local history and ghost story forums."



##########################################################################################

	key[]: "tb_ca2"
	val[]: "The Shasta Route"

	key[]: "tb_ca2_desc"
	val[]: "The Shasta Route winds through pine forests and glimmering alpine lakes beneath Mount Shasta. Roads rise and fall through hidden valleys, carrying travellers south as the mountains gradually fade, giving way to the ancient redwoods of northern California."

	key[]: "poi_ca2_01"
	val[]: "Shasta-Trinity National Forest"

	key[]: "poi_ca2_01_desc"
	val[]: "The Trinity National Scenic Byway winds through Shasta-Trinity National Forest, California’s largest, following routes used by 19th-century gold miners and settlers."

	key[]: "poi_ca2_02"
	val[]: "Big Lagoon"

	key[]: "poi_ca2_02_desc"
	val[]: "Big Lagoon is the largest and southernmost of the three lagoons in Humboldt Lagoons State Park. These shallow coastal basins lie between rocky headlands, where ocean waves have built sandy barriers that separate the lagoons from the Pacific Ocean."

	key[]: "or_cut_sequoia_desc"
	val[]: "Redwood National Park protects some of the world's tallest and oldest coastal redwoods, which reach over 370 feet and can live up to 2,000 years. Together with nearby state parks, it preserves about 35% of the planet’s remaining ancient old-growth redwood forests."
	
	key[]: "poi_ca2_03"
	val[]: "Smith River"

	key[]: "poi_ca2_03_desc"
	val[]: "Within Six Rivers National Forest in north-western California, this protected area was designated by Congress in 1990 to preserve scenic, ecological, and cultural values. The Smith River is California’s only major undammed, free-flowing river."

	key[]: "poi_ca2_04"
	val[]: "Whiskey Creek Bridge"

	key[]: "poi_ca2_04_desc"
	val[]: "Completed in 1961, the Whiskey Creek Bridge is a three-span welded plate girder structure. It is notable as an early example of bridge design employing three different grades of steel, an innovative welding technique at the time."

	key[]: "poi_ca2_05"
	val[]: "Shasta Lake"

	key[]: "poi_ca2_05_desc"
	val[]: "Shasta Lake, California’s largest reservoir, was formed by Shasta Dam on the Sacramento River. Completed in 1945, it provides water, hydroelectric power, and a scenic spot for recreation."

	key[]: "poi_ca2_06"
	val[]: "Mount Shasta"

	key[]: "poi_ca2_06_desc"
	val[]: "From the 1820s onward, Mount Shasta, the second-tallest peak in the Cascade Range, guided travellers along the Siskiyou Trail at its base. That route later evolved into Interstate 5, which now carries thousands to the mountain each year."



##########################################################################################

	key[]: "tb_ca3"
	val[]: "The Sierra Crossing"

	key[]: "tb_ca3_desc"
	val[]: "Beneath the towering Sierra Nevada, the Inn at Benton sits among open valleys and rolling hills. The Sierra Crossing winds along quiet high-desert roads, where distant peaks and rugged ridges create a timeless panorama, inviting travellers to explore the region’s wide-open landscapes."

	key[]: "ca_cut_olmsted_point_desc"
	val[]: "Established in 1890, Yosemite National Park is one of the most visited parks. It is known for Yosemite Falls, the tallest waterfall in North America, extensive trail systems, and giant sequoias over 3,000 years old."

	key[]: "poi_ca3_01"
	val[]: "Tioga Road"

	key[]: "poi_ca3_01_desc"
	val[]: "Tioga Road is an iconic scenic route running through the heart of Yosemite National Park. It is the highest highway pass in California, linking the western and eastern sides of the Sierra Nevada."

	key[]: "poi_ca3_02"
	val[]: "Mono Lake"

	key[]: "poi_ca3_02_desc"
	val[]: "Mono Lake is more than 1 million years old, making it one of the oldest lakes in North America. With no natural outlet, it has accumulated salts and minerals carried by streams flowing from the eastern Sierra Nevada over thousands of years."
	
	key[]: "poi_ca3_03"
	val[]: "Lake Tahoe"

	key[]: "poi_ca3_03_desc"
	val[]: "Lake Tahoe formed roughly 2 million years ago within the Tahoe Basin, with its present shape carved by Ice Age glaciers. It is the largest alpine lake in North America and the second deepest in the United States."

	key[]: "poi_ca3_04"
	val[]: "Eldorado National Forest"

	key[]: "poi_ca3_04_desc"
	val[]: "Eldorado National Forest forms part of a major watershed that provides water to millions of Californians and supports farms producing nearly half of the nation’s fruits and vegetables."

	key[]: "poi_ca3_05"
	val[]: "Inn at Benton Hot Springs"

	key[]: "poi_ca3_05_desc"
	val[]: "Benton, one of Mono County’s oldest towns, was founded by the Paiute people using its hot springs. In the 1860s, it became a stop for settlers and miners. The Inn, built in the 1940s from local pumice, replaced a 1904 hotel with 10 rooms sharing one bath."



##########################################################################################

	key[]: "tb_ca4"
	val[]: "The Sun & Sand"

	key[]: "tb_ca4_desc"
	val[]: "De woestijn van Death Valley strekt zich uit onder een eindeloze hemel, met duinen, grillige pieken en gebarsten vlaktes. Hazen springen over het zonovergoten zand, schedels markeren stille doorgangen en afgelegen wegen kronkelen door een grimmig, onherbergzaam landschap dat iedereen die zich hier waagt op de proef stelt en ontzag inboezemt."

	key[]: "poi_ca4_01"
	val[]: "Lone Pine"

	key[]: "poi_ca4_01_desc"
	val[]: "Lone Pine is a small town in California’s Owens Valley, at the eastern base of the Sierra. Known for its nearby Alabama Hills, it has long served as a gateway for hikers, climbers and filmmakers exploring the surrounding mountains and desert landscapes."

	key[]: "poi_ca4_02"
	val[]: "Death Valley National Park"

	key[]: "poi_ca4_02_desc"
	val[]: "Death Valley is one of America’s most extreme landscapes, the lowest, hottest, driest and tallest in places. Long before 1800s pioneers, Indigenous tribes lived here, hunting and gathering across this harsh yet historically rich region."

	key[]: "poi_ca4_03"
	val[]: "Father Crowley Vista Point"

	key[]: "poi_ca4_03_desc"
	val[]: "This Vista Point is a popular stop for travellers. Father John J. Crowley, the Desert Padre, often paused here. Near the park’s western boundary, it offers striking views of Rainbow Canyon, a colourful formation shaped by ancient volcanic activity."
	
	key[]: "poi_ca4_04"
	val[]: "Mojave Valley Wells"

	key[]: "poi_ca4_04_desc"
	val[]: "At Valley Wells in the Mojave, ancient groundwater discharge deposits record past wetland environments from the Ice Age. These historic wells reveal how groundwater once supported marshes and lush vegetation in an otherwise arid desert."

	key[]: "poi_ca4_05"
	val[]: "The Colorado River Indian Reservation"

	key[]: "poi_ca4_05_desc"
	val[]: "The reservation stretches along the Colorado River in Arizona and California. Established in 1865 for river tribes, it is home to the Mohave, Chemehuevi, Hopi and Navajo peoples and supports farming and community life today."

##########################################################################################

#@}



#+ Idaho TBs and POIs localization.
#@{

    key[]: "tb_id1"
	val[]: "The Treasure Valley"

	key[]: "tb_id1_desc"
	val[]: "Treasure Valley in southwestern Idaho has a rich history shaped by Native American tribes, early fur traders, and Oregon Trail settlers. Its name reflects the area’s resources and opportunities, and it later grew with agriculture, railroads, and regional trade centered around Boise."

	key[]: "poi_id1_01"
	val[]: "Salmon River"

	key[]: "poi_id1_01_desc"
	val[]: "The Salmon River in Idaho stretches 425 miles through a deep, rugged canyon. It earned the nickname “River of No Return” because early boats were unable to travel back upstream against its strong, challenging currents."

	key[]: "poi_id1_02"
	val[]: "Boise National Forest"

	key[]: "poi_id1_02_desc"
	val[]: "Spanning the erodible Idaho Batholith, Boise National Forest features a rugged landscape forged by volcanic activity. This geologic heritage is best seen in its steep canyons and the many natural hot springs tucked throughout the terrain."

	key[]: "poi_id1_03"
	val[]: "Horseshoe Bend"

	key[]: "poi_id1_03_desc"
	val[]: "After gold was found in Boise Basin in 1862, miners wintering by the river founded Horseshoe Bend. Originally called Warrinersville for a local sawmill owner, the town took its current name in 1867 as it became a key stop for prospectors."
	
	key[]: "poi_id1_04"
	val[]: "Toll Gate"

	key[]: "poi_id1_04_desc"
	val[]: "In 1864, a new canyon route replaced the steep Syrup Creek trail. For 20 years, tolls funded its upkeep, while James A. Porter’s famous hotel and dairy became a premier stop for travelers seeking refuge."

	key[]: "poi_id1_05"
	val[]: "Castle Rock"

	key[]: "poi_id1_05_desc"
	val[]: "Toward Camas Prairie, Castle Rock’s granite outcrops served as iconic landmarks for Oregon Trail emigrants on Goodale’s Cutoff. Used from 1862 until the era of cars, these rare formations offered travelers a striking sight along the route."

    key[]: "poi_id1_06"
	val[]: "Duck Valley Indian Reservation"

	key[]: "poi_id1_06_desc"
	val[]: "In 1886, President Cleveland expanded Duck Valley into Idaho via the “Paddy Cap Extension.” By 1934, the various bands that relocated to the reservation gained official federal recognition as the unified Shoshone-Paiute Tribes."


##########################################################################################

    key[]: "tb_id2"
	val[]: "The Magic Valley"

	key[]: "tb_id2_desc"
	val[]: "In the early 1900s, I.B. Perrine turned Idaho’s dry desert into farmland by building the Milner Dam. Completed in 1905, the dam used Snake River water to “magically” transform the landscape. This created the Magic Valley, leading to the thriving farm towns that still exist today."

	key[]: "id_cut_perrine_desc"
	val[]: "Higher than Niagara Falls at 212 feet, Shoshone Falls is a beautiful Snake River landmark located right on the edge of Twin Falls."

	key[]: "id_cut_galena_desc"
	val[]: "Founded in 1879 after a lead-silver discovery, this mining town faded by the 1890s as prices fell. Today, the area thrives as a recreation hub, home to the historic Galena Lodge and the nearby, scenic Galena Summit."

	key[]: "poi_id2_01"
	val[]: "Bison Jump"

	key[]: "poi_id2_01_desc"
	val[]: "Located in Challis, the Bison Jump is a historic site where Indigenous people stampeded buffalo over cliffs for harvesting. Used for over 2,000 years, this strategic landscape offers a glimpse into ancient communal hunting traditions."
	
	key[]: "poi_id2_02"
	val[]: "Grand View Canyon"

	key[]: "poi_id2_02_desc"
	val[]: "Set along the Peaks to Craters Scenic Byway on US-93, Grand View Canyon offers stunning views of the Lost River Range. This mountain chain is home to nine of Idaho’s tallest peaks, making the canyon a highlight of the scenic route."

	key[]: "landmark_id_mackay_rs_desc"
	val[]: "Originally called Goddin’s River for fur trapper Thyery Goddin, the Big Lost River is famous for sinking into lava beds. This “lost” water travels underground, eventually re-emerging at the spectacular Thousand Springs near the Snake River."

    key[]: "poi_id2_03"
	val[]: "Ghost Towns of Mackay"

	key[]: "poi_id2_03_desc"
	val[]: "Based on “Mine Hill” in the White Knob Mining District, towns like White Knob and Cliff Creek fueled a copper, gold, and silver boom until the mid-20th century. These sites remain key remnants of Idaho’s historic mining industry."

	key[]: "id_cut_craters_desc"
	val[]: "Formed by volcanic eruptions 15,000 years ago, Craters of the Moon features a vast landscape of lava flows and cinder cones. Its otherworldly terrain is so unique that NASA astronauts once used it to train for moon missions."



##########################################################################################

#@}



#+ Montana TBs and POIs localization.
#@{

    key[]: "tb_mt1"
	val[]: "The Glacier Passage"

	key[]: "tb_mt1_desc"
	val[]: "Glacier Country is home to some of the last active glaciers in the Rocky Mountains. Over thousands of years, these ancient ice masses carved deep valleys, sharp peaks, and pristine lakes, shaping the landscape that defines western Montana today."

	key[]: "poi_mt1_01"
	val[]: "Kootenai Falls"

	key[]: "poi_mt1_01_desc"
	val[]: "Kootenai Falls is the largest undammed waterfall in Montana and a major regional attraction. The Kootenai River drops about 300 feet over a short distance, creating a powerful, scenic cascade that draws visitors for its unforgettable views."

	key[]: "poi_mt1_02"
	val[]: "Berne Roadside Park"

	key[]: "poi_mt1_02_desc"
	val[]: "Bad Rock Canyon was a popular stop for travelers along the Flathead River, known for fishing, scenic views, and a natural spring once used to cool overheated car radiators. In 1956, it became one of Montana’s first rest areas, named Berne Roadside Park."

	key[]: "poi_mt1_03"
	val[]: "Heavens Peak"

	key[]: "poi_mt1_03_desc"
	val[]: "Heavens Peak, one of Glacier National Park’s most iconic mountains, was named in the 1890s by prospector Louie Meyer. Struck by its beauty and the towering Garden Wall, explorers saw it as unlike anything they had encountered on their journey."

	key[]: "mt_cut_glacier_national_park_desc"
	val[]: "Celebrated as the “Crown of the Continent,” Glacier National Park was established in 1910. Shaped by ancient glaciers, it gained wider access in 1932 with the completion of Going-to-the-Sun Road, now a historic engineering landmark."

	key[]: "poi_mt1_04"
	val[]: "Blackfeet Indian Memorial"

	key[]: "poi_mt1_04_desc"
	val[]: "On the eastern edge of Glacier National Park lies the Blackfeet Nation. Known as the “warriors of the plains,” their name is believed to come from moccasins darkened with ash. Today, several sites in the park remain sacred to the tribe."

	key[]: "mt_cut_flathead_lake_desc"
	val[]: "Flathead Lake is the largest natural freshwater lake by surface area in the contiguous United States. Fed by snowmelt from surrounding mountains, its exceptionally clear water fully renews every few years, making it one of the cleanest large lakes in the world."

	key[]: "mt_cut_thompson_falls_desc"
	val[]: "Named after British-Canadian fur trader and cartographer David Thompson, the town grew after the railroad arrived in 1881. It expanded further during the nearby Coeur d’Alene gold rush, as it became a key stop for miners heading along the Murray Trail."

	key[]: "landmark_mt_missoula_desc"
	val[]: "The Neoclassical Missoula County Courthouse, built of sandstone and designed by A. J. Gibson in 1910, replaced an earlier, smaller structure. Missoula County, formed in 1860, is one of Montana’s oldest political regions, spanning from the Bitterroot to the Rocky Mountains."

    key[]: "poi_mt1_05"
	val[]: "Lolo Creek"

	key[]: "poi_mt1_05_desc"
	val[]: "The corridor between Lolo and Lolo Pass follows a historic route along Lolo Creek. Once traveled by the Nez Perce, early settlers, and the Lewis and Clark Expedition, it remains an important cultural and historical landscape marked by interpretive sites."

##########################################################################################

    key[]: "tb_mt2"
	val[]: "The Great Plains"

	key[]: "tb_mt2_desc"
	val[]: "Montana’s Great Plains stretch across the eastern part of the state, characterized by vast grasslands, rolling prairies, and big open skies. The region has yielded some of North America’s most important dinosaur fossils, offering a glimpse into a landscape that existed millions of years ago."

	key[]: "poi_mt2_01"
	val[]: "Hell Creek Formation"

	key[]: "poi_mt2_01_desc"
	val[]: "The Hell Creek Formation, centered along Hell Creek near Jordan, Montana, preserves an ancient subtropical landscape of rivers, forests, and swamps. Dating to 67–66 million years ago, it is world famous for its wealth of dinosaur fossils, including the iconic Tyrannosaurus rex."

	key[]: "poi_mt2_02"
	val[]: "Yellowstone River"

	key[]: "poi_mt2_02_desc"
	val[]: "The Yellowstone River is the longest free flowing river in the contiguous United States. Rising in Yellowstone National Park, it travels 676 miles through Wyoming and Montana before joining the Missouri River, flowing its entire length without a single dam."

	key[]: "poi_mt2_03"
	val[]: "Glendisaurus"

	key[]: "poi_mt2_03_desc"
	val[]: "Glendisaurus, a life-sized Triceratops statue in Glendive, celebrates the region’s rich dinosaur heritage. Standing about 10 feet tall, it serves as a popular landmark along the Dinosaur Trail."
	
	key[]: "poi_mt2_04"
	val[]: "Missouri River"

	key[]: "poi_mt2_04_desc"
	val[]: "Known as the “Big Muddy,” the Missouri River is the longest river in the United States. Flowing 2,341 miles from Montana’s Rocky Mountains to the Mississippi River, it played a vital role in exploration, trade, and the westward expansion of the American frontier."

	key[]: "mt_cut_fort_peck_spillway_desc"
	val[]: "Fort Peck Dam, built during the Great Depression, is the world’s largest hydraulically filled earth dam. Completed as one of America’s biggest public works projects, it was designed to provide jobs, control floods, and generate hydroelectric power."

    key[]: "poi_mt2_05"
	val[]: "Valley County Pioneer Museum"

	key[]: "poi_mt2_05_desc"
	val[]: "This museum showcases the history of northeastern Montana through exhibits on the Assiniboine people, railroads, agriculture, the Fort Peck Dam, and the Lewis and Clark Expedition. It also houses extensive archives and one of the largest Assiniboine collections in the country."

	key[]: "poi_mt2_06"
	val[]: "Sleeping Buffalo Rock"

	key[]: "poi_mt2_06_desc"
	val[]: "These large glacial boulders were sacred landmarks for Indigenous tribes. Ancient glaciers carved and transported them. The smaller rock, “Buffalo Rock,” was carved to resemble a buffalo, while the larger, “Medicine Rock,” features various petroglyphs."



##########################################################################################

#@}



#+ Wyoming TBs and POIs localization.
#@{

    key[]: "tb_wy1"
	val[]: "The Buffalo Trail"

	key[]: "tb_wy1_desc"
	val[]: "Home to Yellowstone and Grand Teton, it sits on an active volcanic system that powers geysers and hot springs, while glaciers and faults shaped its peaks and valleys. These forces make Northwest Wyoming one of the most geologically dynamic and visually iconic landscapes in the American West."

	key[]: "landmark_wy_yellowsto_desc"
	val[]: "Yellowstone, established in 1872 as the world’s first national park, sits atop a massive dormant volcano. Home to about half of the world’s active geysers, it also protects one of the largest and most intact temperate ecosystems on Earth."

	key[]: "poi_wy1_01"
	val[]: "Lower Geyser Basin"

	key[]: "poi_wy1_01_desc"
	val[]: "Lower Geyser Basin is Yellowstone’s largest geyser basin, featuring a vast concentration of geysers, hot springs, and steam vents. Powered by heat from deep underground magma, it showcases one of the park’s most active geothermal landscapes."

	key[]: "poi_wy1_02"
	val[]: "Yellowstone Lake"

	key[]: "poi_wy1_02_desc"
	val[]: "Yellowstone Lake is North America’s largest high-elevation lake and supports the continent’s largest population of wild cutthroat trout. Scientists believe it once drained toward the Pacific Ocean, allowing fish to cross the Continental Divide through Two Ocean Pass."
	
	key[]: "poi_wy1_03"
	val[]: "Buffalo Bill Dam"

	key[]: "poi_wy1_03_desc"
	val[]: "Completed in 1910, Buffalo Bill Dam was one of the first concrete arch dams in the United States. Standing 325 feet tall, it was the highest dam in the world at the time and became a landmark of early engineering innovation."

	key[]: "poi_wy1_04"
	val[]: "Colters Hell"

	key[]: "poi_wy1_04_desc"
	val[]: "Colter’s Hell is a historic geothermal area along the Shoshone River, named by explorer John Colter in 1807. Early mountain men likened its steaming vents and sulfurous odors to “hell.” Today, only hot springs and steam vents remain active."

	key[]: "wy_cut_grand_teton_desc"
	val[]: "Grand Teton National Park wordt gekenmerkt door de door gletsjers gevormde pieken die steil boven Jackson Hole uitrijzen. De bergen, die ooit door pelsjagers de \"Pilot Knobs\" werden genoemd vanwege hun goede zichtbaarheid over de vlaktes, behoren nog steeds tot de meest iconische bezienswaardigheden van het Amerikaanse Westen."

	key[]: "poi_wy1_05"
	val[]: "Crowheart Butte"

	key[]: "poi_wy1_05_desc"
	val[]: "Crowheart Butte is een prominent herkenningspunt dat vernoemd is naar een veldslag uit 1866 tussen de Shoshone en de Crow-stammen. Volgens de overlevering plaatste opperhoofd Washakie na de overwinning van de Shoshone het hart van een Crow-opperhoofd bovenop de heuvel als waarschuwing, wat de formatie haar blijvende naam gaf."

##########################################################################################

    key[]: "tb_wy2"
	val[]: "The Red Desert"

	key[]: "tb_wy2_desc"
	val[]: "Wyoming’s Red Desert is a vast, high-desert basin defined by wind-sculpted sand, sagebrush plains, and striking red rock formations. One of the largest uninhabited areas in North America, it is shaped by extreme climate, ancient lake beds, and long migration routes."

	key[]: "poi_wy2_01"
	val[]: "Red Canyon"

	key[]: "poi_wy2_01_desc"
	val[]: "Red Canyon was carved during the uplift of the Wind River Range around 60 million years ago. Its distinctive red cliffs are formed from Chugwater rock, whose oxidized iron gives the canyon the vivid color that inspired its name."

	key[]: "poi_wy2_02"
	val[]: "Landscapes of Power"

	key[]: "poi_wy2_02_desc"
	val[]: "Boar’s Tusk, a volcanic rock formation rising over 400 feet above Wyoming’s Red Desert, is the eroded core of a volcano that formed millions of years ago. It overlooks the vast Killpecker Sand Dunes, part of North America’s largest active dune field."

	key[]: "poi_wy2_03"
	val[]: "Rock Springs"

	key[]: "poi_wy2_03_desc"
	val[]: "Rock Springs is known as the “City of 56 Nationalities” for its diverse immigrant heritage. In the late 1800s, people from around the world arrived to work in Union Pacific coal mines. The city is also linked to the famous outlaw Butch Cassidy."
	
	key[]: "poi_wy2_04"
	val[]: "Butch Cassidy Cabin"

	key[]: "poi_wy2_04_desc"
	val[]: "Butch Cassidy was a famous American outlaw who led the “Wild Bunch,” a gang known for train and bank robberies in the Old West. He earned the nickname “Butch” while working in a Rock Springs butcher shop. The most legendary and influential heist was the Wilcox Train Robbery in 1899."

	key[]: "wy_cut_evanston_desc"
	val[]: "Founded in 1869 by the Union Pacific Railroad, Evanston was named after surveyor James A. Evans. The city grew as a railroad hub and later became a regional center for agriculture, oil, and gas."

    key[]: "poi_wy2_05"
	val[]: "Lander Cutoff"

	key[]: "poi_wy2_05_desc"
	val[]: "Built after the Pacific Wagon Road Act of 1857, the Lander Cutoff was one of the first federally funded wagon roads in the American West. Named for engineer Frederick W. Lander, it provided a shorter route for emigrants traveling westward."

	

##########################################################################################

#@}


#+ South Dakota TBs and POIs localization.
#@{

    key[]: "tb_sd1"
	val[]: "The Black Hills"

	key[]: "tb_sd1_desc"
	val[]: "Western South Dakota is defined by the Black Hills and Badlands. Here, vast prairies rise into pine-covered mountains, granite spires, and roaming bison herds. Just beyond, the rugged Badlands reveal a striking landscape carved by wind and water over millions of years."

	key[]: "poi_sd1_01"
	val[]: "Black Hills National Forest"

	key[]: "poi_sd1_01_desc"
	val[]: "Known to the Lakota as Paha Sapa, meaning “Hills That Are Black”, this pine-covered region is home to Black Elk Peak, South Dakota’s highest point at 7,244 feet. Renamed in honor of the famed Lakota healer, it is also the tallest summit east of the Rockies."

	key[]: "sd_cut_rush_desc"
	val[]: "Mount Rushmore, known as America’s “Shrine of Democracy,” was carved into the Black Hills between 1927 and 1941. The monument features 60-foot sculptures of Presidents Washington, Jefferson, Roosevelt, and Lincoln, representing the country’s birth, growth, development and preservation."

	key[]: "poi_sd1_02"
	val[]: "Beaver Creek Bridge"

	key[]: "poi_sd1_02_desc"
	val[]: "Built in 1929, this 225-foot steel and concrete arch bridge spans a canyon near Wind Cave National Park. Rising 115 feet above the canyon floor, it is South Dakota’s only bridge of its kind and was designed to blend seamlessly with the surrounding rock."

	key[]: "sd_cut_sturgis_desc"
	val[]: "Sturgis is known for hosting the world’s largest motorcycle rally. Started in 1938 by local Indian dealer Pappy Hoel, the annual 10-day event brings riders from around the globe to the Black Hills for motorcycle shows, races, music, and celebrations."

	key[]: "sd_cut_deadwood_desc"
	val[]: "Deadwood rose during the Black Hills gold rush of 1876 and became one of the Wild West’s most notorious towns. Designated a National Historic Landmark in 1961, it is the only U.S. city with that status, preserving much of its historic downtown."

	key[]: "sd_cut_badlands_desc"
	val[]: "Badlands National Park spans 244,000 acres of buttes, pinnacles, and spires alongside the nation’s largest protected mixed-grass prairie. It also preserves one of the world’s richest fossil beds, revealing ancient mammals that once roamed the Great Plains."

	key[]: "poi_sd1_03"
	val[]: "Doane Robinson Tunnel"

	key[]: "poi_sd1_03_desc"
	val[]: "Doane Robinson, known as the “Father of Mount Rushmore,” envisioned a grand monument in the Black Hills to attract visitors to South Dakota. Today, his legacy continues, with Mount Rushmore perfectly framed through the scenic Doane Robinson Tunnel."

	key[]: "sd_cut_hot_springs_desc"
	val[]: "Hot Springs, oorspronkelijk Minnekahta genoemd door de Sioux vanwege het \"hete water\", werd in 1879 gesticht rondom minerale bronnen waarvan men geloofde dat ze genezende krachten bezaten. Na een naamsverandering in 1882 ontwikkelde het zich tot een kuuroord en staat het nog steeds bekend om de historische Evans Plunge."

	key[]: "poi_sd1_04"
	val[]: "The Mammoth Site"

	key[]: "poi_sd1_04_desc"
	val[]: "The Mammoth Site is a world-famous Ice Age fossil dig where 61 mammoths have been uncovered. Discovered in 1974, the ancient sinkhole preserves the largest collection of Columbian mammoth remains in North America, with excavations still ongoing today."

##########################################################################################

    key[]: "tb_sd2"
	val[]: "The Great Divide"

	key[]: "tb_sd2_desc"
	val[]: "South Dakota’s Missouri River region marks the state’s central divide, with broad plains on one side and rolling hills on the other. It was a key corridor for Native nations, fur traders, and Lewis and Clark, and later became home to major dams, frontier towns, and rich river history."

	key[]: "poi_sd2_01"
	val[]: "Four Great Lakes"

	key[]: "poi_sd2_01_desc"
	val[]: "Created by dams on the Missouri River in the late 1940s, Lakes Oahe, Sharpe, Francis Case, and Lewis & Clark now define central South Dakota. Together, these reservoirs provide more than 440 miles of shoreline for boating, fishing, and recreation."

	key[]: "poi_sd2_02"
	val[]: "The Ghost Town of Bangor"

	key[]: "poi_sd2_02_desc"
	val[]: "Bangor was once the county seat of Walworth County but became a ghost town after its buildings were moved to nearby Selby in 1899. Today, only a monument marks the site where the town once stood."

	key[]: "landmark_sd_state_capitol_desc"
	val[]: "Founded in 1880, Pierre became South Dakota’s capital when the state joined the Union in 1889. Chosen for its central location and Missouri River crossing, the city was named after French-American fur trader Pierre Chouteau Jr."

	key[]: "sd_cut_corn_desc"
	val[]: "The Corn Palace in Mitchell is the world’s only palace decorated entirely with corn. Each year, local artists and farmers create new murals using naturally colored corn, grains, and grasses, making it one of South Dakota’s most unique landmarks."

	key[]: "landmark_sd_largest_pheasant_desc"
	val[]: "Huron is home to the World’s Largest Pheasant, a 40-foot-tall fiberglass and steel sculpture built in 1959. Weighing 22 tons, it celebrates the region’s long-standing reputation as one of America’s premier pheasant hunting destinations."

	key[]: "landmark_sd_dignity_of_earth_and_sky_desc"
	val[]: "Dignity Of Earth and Sky is een 15 meter hoog roestvrijstalen kunstwerk met uitzicht op de Missouri-rivier bij Chamberlain. Het is gemaakt door kunstenaar Dale Lamphere en eert het erfgoed, de veerkracht en de culturen van de Lakota- en Dakota-bevolking."

##########################################################################################

#@}
